# 993-humanized-typing-scroll-drag.patch
#
# camchrome: the human input engine beyond clicks. 130 added Browser.humanizedClick (a
# Bezier/min-jerk pointer path with tremor and eased timing, routed as real input). This adds
# three browser-level CDP commands on the same principle -- events are built as WebInputEvents
# and routed through the browser's input path, so pages see isTrusted input, no debugger is
# attached to the page, and navigator.webdriver stays false:
#
#   Browser.humanizedType   {targetId, text, cpm?, typoRate?}
#       Per-key RawKeyDown/Char/KeyUp with log-normal inter-key gaps, 40-170 ms dwell and
#       natural rollover; Shift is pressed before and released after capitals and symbols
#       (held across runs of them); longer gaps after spaces, punctuation and newlines; a rare
#       thinking pause; optional neighbouring-key typos noticed and fixed with Backspace.
#       Text outside the US layout is committed as IME text. Keys go to the focused widget,
#       including out-of-process iframes.
#
#   Browser.humanizedScroll {targetId, x?, y?, deltaX?, deltaY, style?}
#       Reaches (x, y) first, then scrolls. style "auto" follows the persona OS: wheel notches
#       for Windows (100 px) and Linux (120 px) in flicks of 2-6 with began/changed/ended
#       phases as Aura sends them; a macOS trackpad gesture (precise deltas, bell-shaped
#       stroke, decaying momentum phase); finger swipes for Android (touch, via 992).
#
#   Browser.humanizedDrag   {targetId, fromX, fromY, toX, toY, duration?}
#       Reach, press, human path with the button held, short hold, release. A touch swipe on
#       an Android persona.
#
# All three continue from where the previous humanized pointer move ended (130's per-target
# cursor), so a script's pointer never teleports.
#
# Tests: tools/network_selftest.py (group "page") types into an input, checks trusted keys,
# Shift on capitals and uneven gaps, corrected typos, a drag onto a target and wheel scrolling.
#
# A key never goes down again before its previous press came up, tracked per key: in "noon" or
# "aba" the key in between can be shorter than the first press's hold.
